{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_62","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0001\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Belmonte Cardozo reiterated that forensic border searches require some individualized suspicion but did not establish whether reasonable suspicion, probable cause, or a warrant is required.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"2026 Alasaad border device forensic search minimum showing reasonable suspicion warrant\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0002\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The latest appellate authority still avoids selecting a precise constitutional threshold for advanced forensic border-device searches.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"United States v Belmonte Cardozo 2026 forensic border search standard\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0003\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Garrett confirmed that circuits remain divided over Brady disclosure before guilty pleas, and the pending Mills petition had not produced a Supreme Court grant by July 21, 2026.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"2025 2026 Brady pre guilty plea circuit split Garrett Mills cert\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0004\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Courts continue to disagree whether Ruiz permits withholding material exculpatory evidence before a plea, with no Supreme Court merits decision resolving the split.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"Brady Ruiz exculpatory evidence pre plea circuit split 2026\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0005\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No later decision displaced al-Kidd's characterization of the pretextual material-witness theory as novel or resolved that theory on the merits.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"Ashcroft v al-Kidd pretext material witness later decision first impression\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0006\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The al-Kidd litigation ended on qualified-immunity grounds without a later ruling deciding Ashcroft's claimed absolute immunity.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"Ashcroft al-Kidd remand absolute immunity decision\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0007\",\"verdict\":\"MOVED\",\"basis\":\"On remand the Fifth Circuit applied the Supreme Court's totality-of-the-circumstances rule and held Felix's shooting constitutionally reasonable.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"Barnes v. Felix, U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit, Sept. 18, 2025, No. 22-20519\",\"discovery_path\":\"Barnes v Felix remand Fifth Circuit 2025 reasonableness\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0008\",\"verdict\":\"MOVED\",\"basis\":\"Hiibel upheld compelled identification during a valid Terry stop and therefore answered the lawful-stop question Brown had reserved.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"Hiibel v. Sixth Judicial District Court of Nevada, U.S. Supreme Court, June 21, 2004, No. 03-5554, 542 U.S. 177\",\"discovery_path\":\"Supreme Court lawful Terry stop require identify Hiibel Brown Texas\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0009\",\"verdict\":\"MOVED\",\"basis\":\"On Brownback remand the Sixth Circuit held that the FTCA judgment bar applied to the Bivens claims asserted in the same action.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"King v. United States, U.S. Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit, Sept. 19, 2022, No. 17-2101, 49 F.4th 991\",\"discovery_path\":\"Brownback v King remand same suit judgment bar Bivens Sixth Circuit\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0010\",\"verdict\":\"MOVED\",\"basis\":\"The Sixth Circuit resolved the alternative same-suit judgment-bar issue that the Supreme Court had declined to decide in Brownback.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"King v. United States, U.S. Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit, Sept. 19, 2022, No. 17-2101, 49 F.4th 991\",\"discovery_path\":\"King United States 49 F.4th 991 judgment bar same lawsuit\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0011\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Recent knock-and-talk cases remain fact-specific, and the pending Reel petition had not produced a Supreme Court grant or nationwide rule for entrances beyond the front door.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"2025 2026 knock and talk entrance beyond front door Reel cert\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0012\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No controlling Supreme Court decision was found defining when a secondary entrance is sufficiently visitor-accessible for the knock-and-talk exception.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"visitor accessible entrance knock and talk Supreme Court cert Reel North Carolina\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0013\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The Supreme Court denied Carter's 2026 petition without deciding whether race may inform whether a reasonable person would feel free to terminate an encounter.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"United States v Carter race free to terminate encounter cert 25-885\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0014\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Chatrie held that geofence acquisition is a search but expressly left probable cause and particularity questions for remand.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"Chatrie Supreme Court 2026 geofence probable cause particularity\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0015\",\"verdict\":\"MOVED\",\"basis\":\"On remand Martinez held that brutal coercive questioning of a seriously wounded suspect could violate clearly established substantive due process.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"Martinez v. City of Oxnard, U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit, 2003, No. 00-56520, 337 F.3d 1091\",\"discovery_path\":\"Chavez Martinez remand substantive due process coercive interrogation 337 F.3d 1091\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0016\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Howell acknowledged Chiaverini's causation problem but expressly left the proper causation standard for another case.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"Chiaverini remand causation Howell McCormick 2025\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0017\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Post-Chiaverini lower courts have applied differing approaches, and no controlling decision has settled causation for multi-charge Fourth Amendment malicious-prosecution claims.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"2025 2026 Chiaverini multi charge malicious prosecution causation\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0018\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Later cases such as Winder applied circuit-specific exigency rules but did not resolve the ADA accommodation question the Supreme Court dismissed in Sheehan.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"Sheehan ADA accommodation armed mentally ill arrest later cases Winder\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0019\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No subsequent Supreme Court merits decision was found addressing whether and when Title II requires accommodation during an armed, exigent police encounter.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"Supreme Court Title II ADA police arrest armed mentally ill accommodation after Sheehan\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0020\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No later Supreme Court decision has supplied the categorical workplace-text privacy expectation that Quon deliberately left unresolved.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"City of Ontario v Quon workplace texts reasonable expectation privacy later Supreme Court\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0021\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The Tenth Circuit's Tahlequah remand implemented qualified immunity without deciding whether the officers committed a constitutional violation.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"City of Tahlequah v Bond remand constitutional violation 2022\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0022\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No Supreme Court or controlling appellate decision was found extending Davis into a general rule for brief field fingerprinting on reasonable suspicion.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"field fingerprinting reasonable suspicion court Davis Mississippi\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0023\",\"verdict\":\"MOVED\",\"basis\":\"Sitz upheld a fixed, nondiscretionary sobriety-checkpoint program and thus supplied the checkpoint ruling Prouse had reserved.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"Michigan Department of State Police v. Sitz, U.S. Supreme Court, June 14, 1990, No. 88-1897, 496 U.S. 444\",\"discovery_path\":\"Prouse reserved fixed traffic checkpoints later Supreme Court Sitz\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0024\",\"verdict\":\"MOVED\",\"basis\":\"On remand the Fourth Circuit treated Dupree's exhaustion contention as a preserved purely legal issue and rejected it on the merits.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"Younger v. Dupree, U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit, June 17, 2024, No. 21-6423\",\"discovery_path\":\"Younger v Dupree remand purely legal exhaustion 2024\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0025\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Later Fazaga proceedings turned on state-secrets and factual developments without deciding the correct interpretation of Section 1806(f).\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"Fazaga 1806(f) Ninth Circuit 2024 Supreme Court 2026 GVR\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0026\",\"verdict\":\"MOVED\",\"basis\":\"On remand West Virginia's highest court affirmed the search based on implied consent and also discussed alternative grounds.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"State v. Flippo, Supreme Court of Appeals of West Virginia, Nov. 27, 2002, No. 30527, 212 W. Va. 560, 575 S.E.2d 170\",\"discovery_path\":\"Flippo remand consent exception State v Flippo 575 S.E.2d 170\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0027\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No later decision in Gonzalez or elsewhere was found resolving the proposed split-second limitation that the Supreme Court declined to reach.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"Gonzalez v Trevino split second Nieves exception later decision 2025 2026\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0028\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Outrageous-government-conduct decisions remain highly fact-specific, and no controlling decision was found holding that government supply of contraband alone violates due process.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"government supplied contraband substantive due process Hampton later court decision\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0029\",\"verdict\":\"MOVED\",\"basis\":\"Opperman subsequently held that a standardized inventory search of a lawfully impounded vehicle is reasonable under the Fourth Amendment.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"South Dakota v. Opperman, U.S. Supreme Court, July 6, 1976, No. 75-76, 428 U.S. 364\",\"discovery_path\":\"Harris inventory search reserved question later Supreme Court Opperman\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0030\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The targeted search found no controlling decision establishing that brief field fingerprinting is categorically permissible on reasonable suspicion.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"field fingerprint scanner detention reasonable suspicion appellate court\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0031\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Modern rapid-identification materials discuss possible reasonableness but do not provide a Supreme Court holding answering Hayes's reserved field-fingerprinting question.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"Hayes Florida field fingerprinting reasonable suspicion later authority\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0032\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No later appellate authority was found defining the limits of a narrowly circumscribed field-fingerprinting procedure conducted without probable cause.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"narrowly circumscribed field fingerprinting procedure probable cause court\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0033\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Hernandez again declined the antecedent extraterritorial Fourth Amendment question while rejecting the requested Bivens remedy.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"Hernandez v Mesa 2020 extraterritorial Fourth Amendment declined\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0034\",\"verdict\":\"MOVED\",\"basis\":\"Buckley held that prosecutors receive no absolute immunity for investigative conduct performed before probable cause exists.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"Buckley v. Fitzsimmons, U.S. Supreme Court, June 24, 1993, No. 91-7849, 509 U.S. 259\",\"discovery_path\":\"Imbler reserved administrative investigative prosecutor immunity Buckley\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0035\",\"verdict\":\"MOVED\",\"basis\":\"Nieves supplied the general probable-cause rule and elements for retaliatory-arrest claims outside Lozman's unusual municipal-policy setting.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"Nieves v. Bartlett, U.S. Supreme Court, May 28, 2019, No. 17-1174, 587 U.S. 391\",\"discovery_path\":\"Lozman retaliatory arrest other contexts elements Nieves\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0036\",\"verdict\":\"MOVED\",\"basis\":\"On Manuel remand the Seventh Circuit held that the claim accrued when the allegedly wrongful detention ended and described the governing Fourth Amendment claim.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"Manuel v. City of Joliet, U.S. Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit, Sept. 12, 2018, No. 14-3022, 903 F.3d 667\",\"discovery_path\":\"Manuel v City of Joliet remand accrual elements 903 F.3d 667\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0037\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No subsequent Supreme Court ruling was found specifying the complete elements or constitutional source of a fabricated-evidence claim after McDonough.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"after McDonough fabricated evidence claim elements Fourth Amendment due process Supreme Court\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0038\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Recent Fourth and Ninth Circuit decisions continue to reflect divergent Fourth Amendment and due-process treatments rather than a resolved source-of-rights rule.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"2024 2026 fabricated evidence Fourth Amendment due process circuit split Harris Ciria\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0039\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Perttu resolved overlapping exhaustion facts through statutory avoidance and expressly did not decide whether the Seventh Amendment independently requires a jury.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"Perttu Seventh Amendment independently jury exhaustion not reached 2025\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0040\",\"verdict\":\"MOVED\",\"basis\":\"Konan held that the FTCA postal exception covers intentional failures to deliver mail and resolved the acknowledged circuit conflict.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"Postal Service v. Konan, U.S. Supreme Court, Feb. 24, 2026, No. 24-351, 609 U.S. ___\",\"discovery_path\":\"Postal Service v Konan decision 2026 FTCA postal exception\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0041\",\"verdict\":\"MOVED\",\"basis\":\"The Supreme Court's merits decision in Konan resolved the First–Second Circuit disagreement over intentional nondelivery under the postal exception.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"Postal Service v. Konan, U.S. Supreme Court, Feb. 24, 2026, No. 24-351, 609 U.S. ___\",\"discovery_path\":\"Konan Supreme Court intentional nondelivery circuit split opinion\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0042\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Porter upheld a limited periodic ALPR deployment while distinguishing comprehensive tracking, leaving denser and longer-term location chronicles unresolved.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"United States v Porter 2026 ALPR comprehensive tracking Harrison Norfolk\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0043\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The Rothgery remand did not decide whether the six-month delay itself constituted a remediable Sixth Amendment violation, and no later controlling answer was found.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"Rothgery remand six month delay remediable violation Fifth Circuit\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0044\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"McArthur addressed a short external restraint but did not decide the reasonableness of the prolonged premises-securing conduct left open in Segura.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"Segura securing premises reasonableness later Supreme Court McArthur\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0045\",\"verdict\":\"MOVED\",\"basis\":\"Davis adopted a clear-statement rule under which an ambiguous reference to counsel does not require officers to stop questioning.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"Davis v. United States, U.S. Supreme Court, June 24, 1994, No. 92-1949, 512 U.S. 452\",\"discovery_path\":\"Smith ambiguous request counsel later answered Davis Supreme Court\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0046\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Federal and state courts remain divided over whether Payton's reason-to-believe standard equals probable cause, and no Supreme Court resolution or grant was found.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"2025 2026 Payton reason to believe probable cause circuit split Brinkley\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0047\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The Eleventh Circuit continues to apply Touset's broader border-search rule, and no Supreme Court decision has resolved the conflict identified by Cano.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"Cano Touset circuit split forensic border search 2025 2026\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0048\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Later Eleventh Circuit authority reaffirmed rather than eliminated the disagreement over suspicion requirements for forensic border searches.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"Pulido United States 133 F.4th 1274 Touset forensic border search cert\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0049\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No later Fifth Circuit decision was found converting Castillo's manual-search holding into a rule for forensic device examinations.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"Fifth Circuit after Castillo forensic border device search reasonable suspicion\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0050\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Castillo's express reservation of the forensic-search standard remains unanswered by controlling Fifth Circuit or Supreme Court authority.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"United States v Castillo 70 F.4th 894 forensic search reserved later\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0051\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Touset remains contrary to Kolsuz and Cano on forensic border-search suspicion, with no Supreme Court merits decision resolving the split.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"Touset Kolsuz Cano circuit split forensic electronic device border\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0052\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The Eleventh Circuit's continued adherence to Touset confirms that the inter-circuit disagreement identified by Cotterman remains live.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"Cotterman Touset forensic border search split 2026\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0053\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Evans affirmed on the inventory-search ground, so its alternative inevitable-discovery theory was never reached and no later same-case ruling altered that disposition.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"United States v Evans 937 F.2d 1534 inevitable discovery not reached\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0054\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Ganias's en banc good-faith disposition expressly avoided deciding the constitutionality of retaining nonresponsive mirrored data, and no later controlling answer was found.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"2025 2026 Ganias retention nonresponsive mirrored data Second Circuit\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0055\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The Second Circuit still lacks a controlling merits rule governing prolonged retention of nonresponsive files copied during execution of a digital warrant.\",\"contrary_authority\":null,\"discovery_path\":\"Second Circuit digital warrant retain nonresponsive data after Ganias\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0056\",\"verdict\":\"MOVED\",\"basis\":\"King upheld a suspicionless search of a violent-felony probationer who had accepted 